{#
	The shop window, as two questions instead of one wall (XIV-6, XIV-140).

	### What was wrong with the wall

	Until XIV-140 this was a single grid of every module the store offers, in the
	catalogue's order, with the ones the customer already had carrying a green
	badge. That is fine at five modules and stops working well short of thirty, for
	three reasons worth separating, because only one of them is the one the ticket
	named:

	  * **The order was the module *key*, and nobody sees keys.** Labels are
	    translated, so key order is not even alphabetical on the screen: in German
	    this build reads Artikel, Kontakte, Rechnungen, Wissen, Bestellungen,
	    Gutscheine. A grid in an order the reader cannot predict is a grid they
	    have to read all of, whatever its size.
	  * **The two questions were interleaved.** An established customer has most of
	    the catalogue, so "what do I already have" and "what could I add" both cost
	    the whole page. Splitting them is free, because the store knew the answer
	    all along.
	  * **The tile said the least useful thing it could.** For an available module
	    it said how many presets it has, which is a number about a choice nobody
	    has started making. What it needs was already computed and was not shown.

	Search is the fourth, and it is deliberately last: it is what you reach for
	once the first three are fixed, not instead of fixing them.

	### The order of the sections, and why theirs comes first

	Theirs, then the offer. It is one compact row at any realistic module count, it
	grounds the reader in their own installation before the shop starts, and it is
	what somebody arriving to check "do we have invoicing" came for. The offer is
	the taller half and is where the page's weight belongs.

	### What is deliberately not here

	**No categories, and no trades.** A module belongs to as many trades as sell it
	(Contacts is in Basic CRM and in Care Home both), so a category on a module
	would have to be a hand-maintained list that a package already implies.
	Grouping by trade is [XIV-139]'s packages seen from this side, and when they
	land they slot in above "modules you can add" without anything here being
	unpicked. The grouping this ticket ships is the one the store already knew:
	theirs, or not theirs yet.

	**No upgrade offers.** [XIV-70]'s "your module has gained a field" lives on the
	module's own settings and must not leak onto this page. *Install something new*
	and *take more of what you already own* are different acts, and a store that
	mixed them would be a store that keeps selling you things you have.
#}
{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{{ 'store.title'|trans }}{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
	{{ include('_topbar.html.twig') }}

	<main class="container page-shell">
		{{ include('_flashes.html.twig') }}

		<h1 class="h3 mb-1">{{ 'store.title'|trans }}</h1>
		<p class="text-body-secondary">{{ 'store.intro'|trans }}</p>

		{#
			A GET form, so a narrowed store is a URL that can be reloaded and sent to
			somebody. Shown at every catalogue size rather than appearing once the
			build passes some count: a page that changes shape as modules are
			published is a page nobody learns, and the count at which a box would
			appear is a number nobody could defend. One input is not furniture.
		#}
		<form method="get" class="row g-2 align-items-end mb-4" role="search">
			<div class="col-sm-6 col-lg-4">
				<label class="form-label small text-body-secondary mb-1" for="store-q">{{ 'store.find'|trans }}</label>
				<input type="search" class="form-control form-control-sm" id="store-q" name="q"
					   value="{{ query }}" placeholder="{{ 'store.find_placeholder'|trans }}">
			</div>
			<div class="col-auto">
				<button class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary" type="submit">{{ 'store.find_submit'|trans }}</button>
			</div>
			{% if query is not empty %}
				<div class="col-auto">
					<a class="btn btn-sm btn-link" href="{{ path('store_index') }}">{{ 'store.find_clear'|trans }}</a>
				</div>
			{% endif %}
		</form>

		{% if owned is empty and available is empty %}
			<div class="alert alert-secondary">
				{#
					Two different nothings. An empty store is a deployment that has
					published nothing yet; an empty result is a word that matched nothing,
					and the way out of that one is the link beside the box.
				#}
				{{ query is empty ? 'store.empty'|trans : 'store.no_matches'|trans({'%query%': query}) }}
			</div>
		{% endif %}

		{% if owned is not empty %}
			<section id="store-yours" class="mb-4" aria-labelledby="store-yours-heading">
				<h2 id="store-yours-heading" class="h5 d-flex align-items-center gap-2 mb-1">
					{{ 'store.yours'|trans }}
					<span class="badge rounded-pill text-bg-secondary">{{ owned|length }}</span>
				</h2>
				<p class="text-body-secondary small">{{ 'store.yours_help'|trans }}</p>

				<div class="row g-2">
					{% for module in owned %}
						{{ include('store/_owned.html.twig', {module: module}) }}
					{% endfor %}
				</div>
			</section>
		{% endif %}

		{% if available is not empty %}
			<section id="store-available" aria-labelledby="store-available-heading">
				<h2 id="store-available-heading" class="h5 d-flex align-items-center gap-2 mb-1">
					{{ 'store.available'|trans }}
					{#
						The count is of what is on the screen, and what is on the screen is
						what `ModuleCatalog::offeredEntries()` returned. A module withheld by
						[XIV-101]'s pricing state is not in that list, so it is not in this
						number either: a count that included one would be telling a customer
						how many modules an operator has not decided about yet (§6.5).
					#}
					<span class="badge rounded-pill text-bg-secondary">{{ available|length }}</span>
				</h2>
				<p class="text-body-secondary small">{{ 'store.available_help'|trans }}</p>

				<div class="row g-3">
					{% for offer in available %}
						{{ include('store/_available.html.twig', {offer: offer, currency: currency}) }}
					{% endfor %}
				</div>
			</section>
		{% elseif owned is not empty and query is empty %}
			<div class="alert alert-secondary">
				{{ 'store.nothing_to_add'|trans }}
			</div>
		{% endif %}
	</main>
{% endblock %}
